## Lesson 60 – Under the Skin

BASIC shields you from memory maps, but serious C64 developers know where everything lives. Screen RAM, colour RAM, BASIC pointers—understanding them lets you debug glitches and prepare for machine code. Let’s build a simple memory scanner and visualiser.

> [📷 suggested: table of addresses printed on-screen]

---

### The One-Minute Tour

- The zero page (addresses 0–255) holds pointers BASIC relies on—changing them blindly crashes everything.
- Screen RAM lives at `1024–2023` by default; colour RAM lives at `55296–56295` (through the I/O mirror).
- BASIC stores program text starting at the address held in pointers `43/44` (`TXTTAB`) and variables after `VARTAB` (`45/46`).

---

### Example Program

```basic
NEW
10 REM *** MEMORY INSPECTOR ***
20 PRINT CHR$(147)
30 GOSUB 500
40 PRINT "KEY POINTERS"
50 PRINT "  TXTTAB:";PEEK(43)+PEEK(44)*256
60 PRINT "  VARTAB:";PEEK(45)+PEEK(46)*256
70 PRINT "  ARYTAB:";PEEK(47)+PEEK(48)*256
80 PRINT "  MEMTOP:";PEEK(55)+PEEK(56)*256
90 PRINT
100 PRINT "SCREEN SAMPLE (ROW 5)"
110 BASE=1024+4*40
120 FOR X=0 TO 39
130 CODE=PEEK(BASE+X)
140 PRINT CHR$(CODE);
150 NEXT X
160 PRINT
170 PRINT "COLOUR SAMPLE"
180 CBASE=55296+4*40
190 FOR X=0 TO 39
200 PRINT FNHEX(PEEK(CBASE+X));" ";
210 NEXT X
220 PRINT
230 PRINT "PRESS RETURN TO HIGHLIGHT POINTERS"
240 INPUT A$
250 GOSUB 600
260 END

500 REM *** HEX OUTPUT UTILITY ***
510 DEF FNHEX(N)=MID$("0123456789ABCDEF",INT(N/16)+1,1)+MID$("0123456789ABCDEF",N-INT(N/16)*16+1,1)
520 RETURN

600 REM *** POINTER VISUALISER ***
610 PRINT CHR$(147)
620 PRINT "POINTER TABLE (ZERO PAGE)"
630 FOR ADD=42 TO 48
640 VAL8=PEEK(ADD)
650 PRINT "";ADD;" ($";FNHEX(ADD);") =";VAL8;" ($";FNHEX(VAL8);")"
660 NEXT ADD
670 PRINT
680 PRINT "THE BASIC PROGRAM BEGINS AT:";PEEK(43)+PEEK(44)*256
690 PRINT "VARIABLES BEGIN AT:";PEEK(45)+PEEK(46)*256
700 RETURN
```

Sample output:

```
KEY POINTERS
  TXTTAB: 2049
  VARTAB: 2117
  ARYTAB: 2117
  MEMTOP: 40959

SCREEN SAMPLE (ROW 5)
READY.2015  COMMODORE 64 BASIC V2

COLOUR SAMPLE
01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01
```

The program prints the addresses stored in BASIC’s zero-page pointers, then dumps a row of screen RAM and colour RAM. `FNHEX` pairs show colour values; `01` is light red by default. Knowing `TXTTAB` (usually 2049) tells you where your program text begins—useful when you start merging or relocating code. The visualiser at the end prints pointer addresses in decimal and hex for quick reference.

> **Tip:** Whenever you `POKE` the memory map, write it down. The VIC-II can relocate screen RAM; if you move it, update your debug tools accordingly.

---

### Experiment Section

- Change the screen base to `$0400` (default). Now `POKE 648,4:POKE 649,0` moves it to `$0400`; rerun the inspector to see pointers update.
- Modify colour RAM (`POKE 55296+i,color`) and rerun to confirm your edits.
- Examine `PEEK(53281)` during gameplay to see which routines change the background colour.
- Create a `PRINTMAP` routine that dumps arbitrary address ranges so you can inspect sprite data or SID registers.

> [🎥 suggested: capture showing pointers updating after relocating screen memory]

---

### Concept Expansion

Understanding the map removes fear. When BASIC misbehaves, you now know where to look: pointers for corrupt programs, screen RAM for visual glitches, colour RAM for palette bugs. This knowledge is mandatory before dropping into machine code.

---

### Game Integration

Keep a debug command that prints pointer values mid-game:

```basic
IF TRACE THEN PRINT "TXTTAB:";PEEK(43)+PEEK(44)*256
```

You’ll spot stray pointers before they corrupt your save table.

